  "content": {
    "id": "p16-q5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "",
    "prompt": "Use the method of the previous question to find the value of $\\cos^{-1}x+\\cos^{-1}(-x)$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\pi$, for $-1\\le x\\le1$.",
      "worked": "For $-1<x<1$,\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\cos^{-1}x+\\cos^{-1}(-x)\\right)&=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\\\\&=0.\\end{aligned}$$\nThe sum is therefore constant. At $x=0$,\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\cos^{-1}(0)+\\cos^{-1}(0)&=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\\\&=\\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\nBy continuity, $\\cos^{-1}x+\\cos^{-1}(-x)=\\pi$ for $-1\\le x\\le1$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 25
  },
